Objective

Build a functional Active Directory home lab environment from scratch — a Domain Controller, a domain-joined Windows 10 client, and an attacker machine — that supports all subsequent Phase 2 labs.

Environment:

Kali Linux (attacker)
Windows Server 2022 (promoted to DC, domain: redteamlab.local)
Windows 10 (domain-joined client)
Oracle VirtualBox, Host-Only Adapter (vboxnet0, 192.168.56.0/24)
What Was Built
Windows Server 2022 installed and configured as a Domain Controller
Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) role installed and promoted
Domain redteamlab.local created
DNS configured on the DC (required for domain functionality)
Windows 10 client joined to the domain
Lab user accounts created: Administrator, jdoe, svc_backup, asrep
Static IPs assigned: DC .101, Client .15, Kali .1 (or host IP)

Key Concepts:

A Domain Controller is a server running AD DS that authenticates users, enforces policy, and manages the domain database (NTDS.dit)
DNS is mandatory for AD — clients find the DC by querying DNS SRV records (_ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.<domain>)
Domain joining creates a computer account in AD and establishes a machine password shared between the workstation and the DC
The Administrator account created during DC promotion is the built-in Domain Admin
Key Lessons
AD DNS must point to the DC itself — external DNS breaks domain functionality
Computer accounts (MACHINE$) are created automatically on domain join
The DC is the single most valuable target in any AD environment — it holds NTDS.dit, the krbtgt key, and all secrets

Interview Explanation:

"I built a self-contained Active Directory lab using VirtualBox with a Windows Server 2022 Domain Controller, a Windows 10 domain-joined client, and a Kali Linux attacker on an isolated host-only network. This gave me a realistic enterprise-style environment to practice every phase of AD offensive security."
